export const articleSources: ArticleSource[] = [
  {
    "id": "choosing-a-water-tank-cleaning-service.mdx",
    "raw": "---\ntitle: How to choose a water tank cleaning service\nslug: choosing-a-water-tank-cleaning-service\ndescription: Questions worth asking before you hire anyone to clean your water tank or sump, and the answers that should give you confidence.\nauthor: tank-sudda-team\npublishedAt: 2026-10-12\ncategory: choosing-a-service\ntags:\n  - hiring\n  - booking\n  - commercial\nrelatedServices:\n  - water-tank-deep-cleaning\n  - commercial-tank-cleaning\nrelatedArticles:\n  - how-to-prepare-for-water-tank-cleaning\ndraft: false\n---\n\nYour water tank supplies the water your household or building uses every day, so it is worth choosing carefully who cleans it. These questions will help you compare services, including ours.\n\n## 1. What exactly is included?\n\nAsk for a clear description of the work. For example:\n\n- Is the tank fully drained, or is only the sediment removed?\n- Are the walls and the underside of the lid cleaned, or only the floor?\n- Where does the sediment go? Is it removed from the tank, or flushed down the outlet?\n- Is the sump included if you have one?\n\nA good provider explains exactly what their clean includes (for example whether the tank is fully drained and scrubbed, as in a [deep clean](/services/water-tank-deep-cleaning)) and recommends what your tank actually needs.\n\n## 2. How is the price worked out?\n\nPrices vary because tanks vary. It is reasonable for a provider to ask about capacity, number of tanks, access and condition before quoting. What matters is that you receive a clear quotation **before** work starts, and that you understand what it covers.\n\nBe cautious of a price that seems unusually low if it is not clear what is included.\n\n## 3. How long will the water be off?\n\nA provider should be able to tell you, at least approximately, how long your supply will be interrupted. This is especially important for apartment buildings, offices, schools and hotels, where the interruption affects many people.\n\n## 4. How do they work safely?\n\nTanks and sumps are enclosed spaces with limited access. Ask how the team approaches safety, especially for sumps and large tanks. A provider who takes the question seriously is more likely to take care with your property too.\n\n## 5. Will they tell you what they found?\n\nA useful clean includes honest feedback: a cracked lid, gaps that let in debris, corroded fittings or a pump that may need attention. You should be told about these so you can decide what to do, rather than being pressured into repairs on the spot.\n\n## 6. Can they handle larger sites?\n\nIf you manage a building or organisation, ask whether the provider can survey the site, prepare a written scope and plan the work around occupants. Our [commercial and institutional service](/services/commercial-tank-cleaning) is built around exactly that kind of planning.\n\n## 7. Is feedback from other customers genuine?\n\nReviews can help, but look for feedback that is specific and attributable. Be wary of vague praise that could apply to any business.\n\n## Summary checklist\n\n- A clear explanation of what is included\n- A quotation before work begins\n- An estimate of how long water will be off\n- A sensible approach to safety\n- Honest reporting of anything found\n- Planning support for larger sites\n\nIf you would like to ask us any of these questions, [get in touch](/contact) or [request a booking](/book).\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "how-often-should-you-clean-your-water-tank.mdx",
    "raw": "---\ntitle: How often should you clean your water tank?\nslug: how-often-should-you-clean-your-water-tank\ndescription: There is no single cleaning interval that suits every tank. Here are the factors that matter and how to judge the right schedule for your property.\nauthor: tank-sudda-team\npublishedAt: 2026-10-12\ncategory: maintenance\ntags:\n  - cleaning-frequency\n  - maintenance\n  - inspection\nrelatedServices:\n  - water-tank-deep-cleaning\n  - sump-cleaning\nrelatedArticles:\n  - water-tank-maintenance-checklist\n  - understanding-sediment-in-water-tanks\ndraft: false\n---\n\nIt is one of the most common questions we hear, and the honest answer is: it depends on your tank. Two houses on the same road can need very different schedules. This guide explains what affects the interval and how to work out a sensible routine for your property.\n\n## Why there is no single answer\n\nA water tank collects material gradually. How quickly that happens depends on a few things that differ from property to property:\n\n- **The incoming supply.** Some supplies carry more fine particles than others, and this can vary through the year.\n- **How well the tank is sealed.** A loose or cracked lid lets in dust, leaves, insects and rainwater run-off.\n- **How much water is used.** A tank that is filled and emptied often moves more water, and more particles, through it.\n- **The tank's age and material.** Older tanks and rough internal surfaces can hold deposits more easily.\n- **Whether there is a sump.** If water passes through an underground sump first, the condition of the sump affects the overhead tank too.\n\nBecause these factors combine differently in each home, a fixed rule can leave one tank cleaned too rarely and another cleaned more often than it needs.\n\n## A practical starting point\n\nMany owners plan a clean at least once a year and then adjust based on what they see. If the tank is found to be in poor condition at that clean, shorten the interval. If it is consistently in good shape, the routine may be working well.\n\nKeep a simple record of when the tank was cleaned and what condition it was in. After two or three cleans you will have a much better idea of the right interval than any general rule can give you.\n\n## Signs your tank may need attention sooner\n\nBetween scheduled cleans, look out for:\n\n- Cloudy or discoloured water at the tap, especially after the tank refills\n- Grit or particles in taps, showers or filters\n- An unusual smell from the water\n- A damaged, missing or poorly fitting lid\n- Visible sediment, slime or staining when you look inside the tank\n\nAny of these is a good reason to inspect the tank and consider bringing the next clean forward. If you are ever concerned about whether your water is safe to drink, seek advice from your water supplier or local public health authorities.\n\n## Deep cleaning as part of a routine\n\nA [deep clean](/services/water-tank-deep-cleaning) empties the tank, removes the settled sediment and scrubs the internal surfaces. It is the right choice for a first clean or a neglected tank, and as a regular service once you know how quickly sediment builds up in your property.\n\n## Don't forget the sump\n\nIf your property has an underground sump, include it in your schedule. Cleaning the overhead tank while the sump stays dirty means sediment can be pumped straight back up. See our [sump cleaning service](/services/sump-cleaning) for more detail.\n\n## Summary\n\n- There is no universal interval; it depends on your supply, the tank and how it is used.\n- Plan a regular clean, keep a record, and adjust based on what you find.\n- Inspect between cleans and act on warning signs.\n- Clean the sump and the overhead tank together where possible.\n\nIf you are not sure where to start, [request a booking](/book) and we can suggest an interval after seeing your tank.\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "how-to-prepare-for-water-tank-cleaning.mdx",
    "raw": "---\ntitle: How to prepare for a water tank cleaning\nslug: how-to-prepare-for-water-tank-cleaning\ndescription: A short checklist to help the day of your tank or sump cleaning go smoothly, from storing water to clearing access to the tank.\nauthor: tank-sudda-team\npublishedAt: 2026-10-12\ncategory: cleaning-explained\ntags:\n  - preparation\n  - booking\n  - sumps\nrelatedServices:\n  - water-tank-deep-cleaning\n  - sump-cleaning\nrelatedArticles:\n  - choosing-a-water-tank-cleaning-service\ndraft: false\n---\n\nA little preparation makes a tank cleaning quicker, safer and less disruptive. Here is what to do before the team arrives.\n\n## A few days before\n\n### Confirm the details\n\nMake sure the booking details are right: the number of tanks, whether a sump is included, and the address. If anything has changed since you sent your request, let us know.\n\n### Find out the tank capacity\n\nThe capacity is often printed or moulded on plastic tanks (for example \"1000 L\"). If you cannot find it, an approximate size is fine. Just tell us roughly how tall and wide the tank is.\n\n### Tell your household or occupants\n\nIf you live in an apartment building or manage an office, give people notice that water will be interrupted. For shared buildings, agree the timing with management first.\n\n## The day before\n\n### Store water for essential use\n\nYour supply will be interrupted while the tank is drained, cleaned and refilled. Fill bottles or containers with enough water for drinking, cooking and essential washing.\n\n### Use water from the tank\n\nIf practical, let the water level drop a little by using it normally. Less water to drain means less wasted water.\n\n### Clear the access route\n\nCheck the route to the tank or sump:\n\n- Move items stored around the tank, on the roof, or over the sump cover.\n- Make sure ladders, stairs or hatches are usable.\n- Secure pets away from the work area.\n\n## On the day\n\n### Show the team the key points\n\nIt helps if someone at the property can point out:\n\n- The main valve that feeds the tank\n- The pump switch or controller (if there is a sump)\n- Any outlets or valves that should not be touched\n\n### Mention any known issues\n\nLet the team know about leaks, a cracked lid, a pump that has been unreliable, or anything else you have noticed. It helps them plan and avoid surprises.\n\n## After the cleaning\n\nOnce the tank refills, it is normal for the first water to be a little cloudy as the system settles. Run your taps briefly. If you notice anything unusual afterwards, contact us.\n\n> **Safety reminder:** Please do not enter a tank or sump yourself, before or after the cleaning. These are enclosed spaces and need the right equipment and precautions.\n\nReady to book? Use the [booking form](/book), or read about our [deep cleaning](/services/water-tank-deep-cleaning) and [sump cleaning](/services/sump-cleaning) services first.\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "plastic-vs-concrete-water-tanks.mdx",
    "raw": "---\ntitle: Plastic vs concrete water tanks - how their care differs\nslug: plastic-vs-concrete-water-tanks\ndescription: Plastic and concrete tanks collect sediment in similar ways but age differently. What to check on each type, and how cleaning differs between them.\nauthor: tank-sudda-team\npublishedAt: 2026-10-12\ncategory: maintenance\ntags:\n  - maintenance\n  - inspection\n  - water-tanks\nrelatedServices:\n  - water-tank-deep-cleaning\n  - commercial-tank-cleaning\nrelatedArticles:\n  - water-tank-maintenance-checklist\n  - understanding-sediment-in-water-tanks\ndraft: true\n---\n\nMost homes and buildings in Sri Lanka store water in either a moulded plastic tank or a built concrete tank (often an underground sump). Both need regular cleaning, but they age differently, so the things worth checking are not quite the same.\n\n## Plastic tanks\n\nMoulded plastic tanks are common on rooftops and stands. They are light, quick to install and have smooth internal walls.\n\n### What to check\n\n- **The lid.** Plastic lids can crack, warp in the sun or lose their fit. A loose lid is the most common way dust, leaves and insects get in.\n- **Sun exposure.** Over years in direct sun, plastic can become brittle. Look for cracks around the inlet, outlet and lid.\n- **The stand.** Make sure the base is level and solid. A full 1,000 L tank weighs around a tonne, so a weak stand is a safety risk.\n- **Fittings.** Check that the inlet and outlet connections are not leaking or cracked.\n\n### Cleaning\n\nSmooth plastic walls do not hold deposits as easily as rough surfaces, but sediment still settles on the floor and a film can form on the walls. Plastic should be cleaned with methods that will not scratch or damage the surface, because scratches give deposits more to cling to.\n\n## Concrete tanks and sumps\n\nConcrete is used for larger tanks and for most underground sumps. It is strong and long-lasting, but its surface is rougher than plastic.\n\n### What to check\n\n- **Cracks and seepage.** Look for cracks in walls you can see, damp patches around the outside, or water levels that drop when no one is using water.\n- **The cover.** Underground sumps are especially exposed to surface water and debris if the cover is damaged, poorly fitting or not raised above the surrounding ground.\n- **Surface condition.** Over time the internal surface can wear and become rougher, which makes it easier for deposits to build up.\n\n### Cleaning\n\nRough concrete surfaces tend to hold more deposits than plastic, so a thorough [deep clean](/services/water-tank-deep-cleaning) with surface scrubbing is usually needed rather than sediment removal alone. Large concrete tanks in apartment buildings and other organisations usually need planning around the people who depend on them; see our [commercial and institutional service](/services/commercial-tank-cleaning).\n\n## Which is easier to look after?\n\nNeither is maintenance-free. A plastic tank is usually easier to inspect and clean; a concrete tank lasts longer but needs closer attention to cracks and covers. Whichever you have, the basics are the same:\n\n1. Keep the lid or cover secure and in good condition.\n2. Inspect from outside regularly (see our [maintenance checklist](/blog/water-tank-maintenance-checklist)).\n3. Clean on a regular schedule, and include the sump if water passes through one.\n\n> **Safety reminder:** Never enter a tank or sump to inspect or clean it yourself. Look from outside, and leave work inside to people who are properly equipped.\n\nNot sure what your tank needs? Describe it in the [booking form](/book) and we will advise.\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "signs-your-sump-needs-cleaning.mdx",
    "raw": "---\ntitle: Signs your sump needs cleaning\nslug: signs-your-sump-needs-cleaning\ndescription: An underground sump is easy to forget because you rarely see inside it. Here are the signs, visible from outside, that it is time for a clean.\nauthor: tank-sudda-team\npublishedAt: 2026-10-12\ncategory: maintenance\ntags:\n  - sumps\n  - inspection\n  - maintenance\nrelatedServices:\n  - sump-cleaning\n  - water-tank-deep-cleaning\nrelatedArticles:\n  - understanding-sediment-in-water-tanks\n  - how-often-should-you-clean-your-water-tank\ndraft: true\n---\n\nMany Sri Lankan homes and buildings store water in an underground sump and pump it up to an overhead tank. Because the sump is below ground and covered, it often goes years without anyone looking inside. These are the signs that it needs attention.\n\n## You have cleaned the overhead tank, but problems come back\n\nIf the overhead tank was cleaned recently but cloudy water, grit or sediment returns quickly, the sump is a likely source. Sediment in the sump is pumped straight back up into the clean tank.\n\n## Grit or particles at the taps\n\nFine sand or particles in taps, shower heads or filters can come from sediment being stirred up and carried through the system. It is worth checking both the sump and the overhead tank.\n\n## The pump is working harder\n\nIf the pump runs longer than it used to, cycles more often, or sounds strained, sediment and debris near the pump intake may be part of the problem. A pump technician can check the pump itself; a clean removes the material around it.\n\n## The cover is damaged or poorly fitting\n\nA sump cover that is cracked, broken, loose or level with the surrounding ground lets in dust, leaves, insects and surface water during rain. If the cover has been in poor condition for a while, the sump is likely to need cleaning, and the cover should be repaired.\n\n## Surface water flows near the sump\n\nIf rainwater runs or pools over the sump area, it can carry dirt in around a poorly sealed cover. After heavy rain, check whether water collects near the cover.\n\n## It has simply been a long time\n\nIf you cannot remember when the sump was last cleaned, or have just moved into a property, it is sensible to have it cleaned and inspected so you know its condition.\n\n## What to do next\n\n1. Do not enter the sump. It is an enclosed space with limited air and access.\n2. From outside, note the condition of the cover and anything unusual about your water.\n3. Plan to clean the sump and the overhead tank together, so clean water is not stored in a dirty system.\n\nRead more about [sump cleaning](/services/sump-cleaning), or [request a booking](/book) and mention that you have a sump.\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "understanding-sediment-in-water-tanks.mdx",
    "raw": "---\ntitle: Understanding sediment in water tanks\nslug: understanding-sediment-in-water-tanks\ndescription: What the layer at the bottom of your tank is made of, how it builds up, why it matters, and what you can do to slow it down.\nauthor: tank-sudda-team\npublishedAt: 2026-10-12\ncategory: cleaning-explained\ntags:\n  - sediment\n  - maintenance\n  - water-tanks\nrelatedServices:\n  - water-tank-deep-cleaning\n  - sump-cleaning\nrelatedArticles:\n  - how-often-should-you-clean-your-water-tank\ndraft: false\n---\n\nIf you have ever looked inside a water tank that has not been cleaned for a while, you have probably seen a layer of fine, soft material on the floor. That layer is sediment. This article explains where it comes from and why it is worth removing.\n\n## What is sediment?\n\nSediment is the general name for solid particles that settle out of stored water. In a domestic tank it is usually a mix of:\n\n- Fine silt, sand and clay carried in by the supply\n- Rust or scale from pipes and fittings\n- Dust, leaves and debris that enter through gaps in the lid\n- Organic matter that can accumulate over time\n\nThe exact mix varies from property to property. It depends on the supply, the condition of the pipework and how well the tank is sealed.\n\n## How it builds up\n\nWhen water sits still in a tank, heavier particles slowly sink to the bottom. Each time the tank refills, more particles arrive and the layer gets a little deeper. The process is gradual, which is why sediment often goes unnoticed until it is quite thick.\n\nIncoming water can stir up the layer as the tank fills. This is one reason some people notice cloudy water or grit at the tap shortly after a tank refills.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nA sediment layer:\n\n- Can be stirred up and carried into your plumbing, taps and appliances\n- Can clog filters, shower heads and fittings over time\n- Makes it harder to keep the inside of the tank clean\n- Hides the tank floor, so cracks or damage are harder to spot\n\nRemoving sediment regularly keeps the tank easier to maintain and helps you see its real condition.\n\n## Can I flush it out myself?\n\nIt is tempting to open the outlet and let the sediment drain away. The problem is that the outlet usually feeds your plumbing, so flushing pushes the material into the pipes you are trying to protect. Professional cleaning lifts sediment *out of the tank* instead.\n\nFor safety reasons, please do not climb into a tank or sump to clean it yourself. These are enclosed spaces, and working inside them needs the right equipment and precautions.\n\n## Slowing it down\n\nYou cannot stop sediment completely, but you can slow it down:\n\n1. Keep the lid closed, secure and in good condition.\n2. Make sure overflow and vent openings are screened.\n3. Keep the area around the tank and sump clean.\n4. Fix leaking or corroded fittings promptly.\n5. Clean the sump as well as the overhead tank.\n\n## Which service removes sediment?\n\nA [deep clean](/services/water-tank-deep-cleaning) drains the tank, lifts the settled sediment out and scrubs the internal surfaces. If your tank is filled from an underground sump, include [sump cleaning](/services/sump-cleaning) as well. If you are not sure what you need, describe your tank in the [booking form](/book) and we will advise.\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "water-tank-care-after-flooding.mdx",
    "raw": "---\ntitle: What to do with your water tank after flooding\nslug: water-tank-care-after-flooding\ndescription: If floodwater may have reached your sump or tank, treat the stored water with caution. Practical steps for checking your tank after heavy rain or flooding.\nauthor: tank-sudda-team\npublishedAt: 2026-10-12\ncategory: cleaning-explained\ntags:\n  - sumps\n  - inspection\n  - water-tanks\nrelatedServices:\n  - sump-cleaning\n  - water-tank-deep-cleaning\nrelatedArticles:\n  - signs-your-sump-needs-cleaning\n  - how-to-prepare-for-water-tank-cleaning\ndraft: true\n---\n\nHeavy monsoon rain and flooding can affect water storage, especially underground sumps. This guide explains what to check and how to plan a clean. It is general guidance, not a substitute for advice from the authorities.\n\n## Put safety first\n\nIf floodwater may have entered your sump or tank, **do not drink the stored water** until you have advice from your water supplier or local public health authorities. Follow any official notices for your area.\n\nElectrical equipment such as pumps should only be handled once it is safe to do so. If a pump or its wiring has been under water, have it checked by a qualified electrician before use.\n\n## How floodwater gets in\n\n- **Through the sump cover**, if the cover is level with the ground, damaged or not sealed.\n- **Through cracks** in the sump walls when the surrounding ground is saturated.\n- **Through overflow or vent openings** that sit low enough for water to reach them.\n\nOverhead tanks are less likely to be reached directly, but they are filled from the sump. If the sump is affected, the overhead tank may be too.\n\n## What to check once the water has gone down\n\n1. Look at the sump cover and the area around it for mud, debris or signs that water stood over it.\n2. Note whether your tap water looks cloudy, discoloured or smells unusual.\n3. Check for new cracks or damage around the sump and tank.\n4. Write down what you see. It helps when you arrange a clean.\n\nDo not enter the sump to look. It is an enclosed space, and after flooding it may contain mud, debris and poor air.\n\n## Plan a clean of the whole system\n\nAfter flooding, it is sensible to clean both the [sump](/services/sump-cleaning) and the [overhead tank](/services/water-tank-deep-cleaning), so that sediment and debris are removed from the whole storage system. Tell us in the [booking form](/book) that the property was affected by flooding, so we can plan the work.\n\n## Reduce the risk next time\n\n- Raise the sump cover above the surrounding ground where possible.\n- Repair cracks and replace damaged covers.\n- Make sure rainwater drains away from the sump area.\n- Keep overflow and vent openings screened and above likely flood levels.\n\n> **Important:** This article gives general guidance only. Always follow the advice of your water supplier and public health authorities about whether water is safe to use.\n"
  },
  {
    "id": "water-tank-maintenance-checklist.mdx",
    "raw": "---\ntitle: A simple water tank maintenance checklist\nslug: water-tank-maintenance-checklist\ndescription: Easy checks you can do from outside the tank to keep it in good condition between professional cleans, and when to call for help.\nauthor: tank-sudda-team\npublishedAt: 2026-10-12\ncategory: maintenance\ntags:\n  - maintenance\n  - inspection\n  - sumps\nrelatedServices:\n  - water-tank-deep-cleaning\n  - sump-cleaning\nrelatedArticles:\n  - how-often-should-you-clean-your-water-tank\n  - understanding-sediment-in-water-tanks\ndraft: false\n---\n\nProfessional cleaning removes what has built up inside a tank, but regular checks in between help keep it in good condition for longer. None of the checks below require you to enter the tank.\n\n## Every month\n\n### Look at the lid\n\nThe lid is the tank's first line of defence. Check that it is:\n\n- In place and fully closed\n- Free from cracks or broken edges\n- Secured so wind cannot lift it\n\nA gap of even a few centimetres can let in dust, leaves, insects and rainwater.\n\n### Check for leaks\n\nLook around the tank base, inlet and outlet connections for drips, damp patches or staining. Small leaks waste water and can get worse over time.\n\n### Notice the water at the tap\n\nPay attention to whether the water looks clear and smells normal. Cloudiness, grit or an unusual smell is a sign to inspect the tank sooner.\n\n## Every few months\n\n### Inspect the overflow and vents\n\nOverflow pipes and vents should have a screen or mesh in place so insects and small animals cannot get in. Replace damaged mesh.\n\n### Look inside from the opening\n\nWith the lid open and from a safe position outside the tank, use a torch to look at the floor and walls. Note whether you can see a sediment layer, staining or slime. Take a photo so you can compare next time.\n\n### Check the sump cover\n\nIf you have an underground sump, make sure the cover is intact, fits well and is not exposed to surface water running into it during rain.\n\n## After heavy rain or supply interruptions\n\nSupply interruptions and heavy rain can sometimes bring more particles into the system. If the water looks different afterwards, give it time to settle and check the tank. Persistent cloudiness is worth investigating.\n\n## Keep a simple record\n\nWrite down:\n\n- When the tank and sump were last cleaned\n- What condition they were in\n- Any repairs made (for example a new lid or fittings)\n\nThis helps you judge [how often your tank needs cleaning](/blog/how-often-should-you-clean-your-water-tank).\n\n## When to call a professional\n\nBook a clean if you notice:\n\n- A visible layer of [sediment](/blog/understanding-sediment-in-water-tanks)\n- Slime, staining or growth on internal surfaces\n- Ongoing cloudy water or grit at the taps\n- That it has been a long time since the last clean\n\nA [deep clean](/services/water-tank-deep-cleaning) removes settled sediment and cleans the internal surfaces. Remember to include the [sump](/services/sump-cleaning) as well.\n\n> **Safety reminder:** Never enter a tank or sump yourself. Inspect from outside, and leave work inside to people who are properly equipped.\n"
  }
];
